<header><p class="st-kicker">principality · Mesopotamia and Iran</p><h1 class="st-title">Baban emirate</h1><p class="st-meta">1649 CE – 1850 CE (approx.) · capital Sulaymaniyah</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Kurdish emirate that founded the city of Sulaymaniyah and became a cradle of Sorani Kurdish literature between the Ottoman and Persian empires.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Baban family rose in the mountains of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shahrizor">Shahrizor</a> in the 17th century, recognised by the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> as hereditary lords. Their early centre was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qalacholan">Qalacholan</a>. In 1784 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibrahim-pasha-baban">Ibrahim Pasha Baban</a> founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sulaymaniyah">Sulaymaniyah</a>, named after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluk</a> governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buyuk-suleyman-pasha">Büyük Süleyman Pasha</a>, as his new capital.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">emirate</a> was a frontier state caught between <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/mamluk-iraq">Mamluk Iraq</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/qajar-iran">Qajar Iran</a>, each of which tried to choose its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emir">emirs</a>; family members often appealed to one side against the other. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sulaymaniyah">Sulaymaniyah</a> became a centre of learning, where poets such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nali-poet">Nali</a>, Salim and Kurdi wrote in Sorani Kurdish rather than Persian, founding a new literary tradition. The economy rested on agriculture, livestock and trade between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>As the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> centralised, they deposed the last emir, Ahmad Pasha, in 1850 and placed the region under a governor. The family remained influential in Kurdish politics.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="principality, 1649 CE – 1850 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M20 74 H80 V60 H20 Z"/><path d="M20 60 Q35 46 50 60 Q65 46 80 60"/><circle cx="20" cy="54" r="3"/><circle cx="35" cy="48" r="3"/><circle cx="50" cy="44" r="3"/><circle cx="65" cy="48" r="3"/><circle cx="80" cy="54" r="3"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">principality</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Mesopotamia and Iran</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:92.52%;width:4.00%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1649 CE – 1850 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>principality</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Sulaymaniyah</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Ibrahim Pasha Baban, Abdurrahman Pasha, Ahmad Pasha</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Kurdish (Sorani)</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/iraq">Iraq</a>, <a href="/countries/iran">Iran</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Baban, Babans, Emirate of Baban</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1750&amp;focus=baban-emirate" aria-label="Baban emirate on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5828 893 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Baban emirate lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6148 1149L5709 970"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5709 970h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6148 1149h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6148 1149h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Sulaymaniyah. <a href="/atlas?year=1750&amp;focus=baban-emirate">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

